/// Adds a BACnet object to an existing device.
///
/// WHO SERVES WHAT, for every object type created this way.
///
/// Handled and stored by the CAS BACnet Stack:
/// - ObjectIdentifier, ObjectType, PropertyList
/// - StatusFlags - the stack ASSEMBLES the bitstring, but reads EventState, Reliability and
/// OutOfService back through your callbacks to do it
/// - PriorityArray and CurrentCommandPriority for a commandable object - also assembled from
/// your callbacks, see below
///
/// Everything else is served by your callbacks. ObjectName is the one every object needs: without
/// CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString the stack answers the literal string "undefined".
///
/// PresentValue is dispatched by the object type's datatype. Register the matching pair:
///
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Property Datatype Callback
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Real analogInput, CallbackGetPropertyReal
/// analogOutput, CallbackSetPropertyReal
/// analogValue,
/// loop, pulseConverter
/// Enumerated binaryInput, CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated
/// binaryOutput, CallbackSetPropertyEnumerated
/// binaryValue,
/// lifeSafetyPoint,
/// lifeSafetyZone,
/// accessDoor
/// Unsigned multiStateInput, CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// multiStateOutput, CallbackSetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// multiStateValue,
/// positiveIntegerValue,
/// accumulator, command
/// Signed integerValue CallbackGetPropertySignedInteger
/// CallbackSetPropertySignedInteger
/// Double largeAnalogValue CallbackGetPropertyDouble
/// CallbackSetPropertyDouble
/// Boolean calendar CallbackGetPropertyBool
/// CallbackSetPropertyBool
/// CharacterString characterstringValue
/// CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString
/// CallbackSetPropertyCharacterString
/// OctetString octetstringValue CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// CallbackSetPropertyOctetString
/// BitString bitstringValue CallbackGetPropertyBitString
/// CallbackSetPropertyBitString
/// Date dateValue, CallbackGetPropertyDate
/// datepatternValue CallbackSetPropertyDate
/// Time timeValue, CallbackGetPropertyTime
/// timepatternValue CallbackSetPropertyTime
/// DateTime datetimeValue, CallbackGetPropertyDate + CallbackGetPropertyTime
/// datetimepatternValue
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
///
/// COMMANDABLE OBJECTS ARE READ DIFFERENTLY. analogOutput, binaryOutput, multiStateOutput,
/// accessDoor, lightingOutput and binaryLightingOutput are always commandable; the *Value types
/// become commandable once you enable BOTH PriorityArray and RelinquishDefault. For a commandable
/// object the stack NEVER calls the plain PresentValue callback. It reads the priority array
/// instead - CallbackGetPropertyBool for the "is this slot NULL" flag, then the typed getter
/// above
/// for the value - both with propertyIdentifier = PriorityArray, useArrayIndex = true and index
/// 1..16, and it reports the highest-priority non-NULL slot, falling back to RelinquishDefault.
/// See BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackGetPropertyBool for that contract.
///
/// DO NOT USE THIS FUNCTION for the object types that have their own constructor. Creating one of
/// these with BACnetStack_AddObject alone produces an object with no engine behind it:
/// - notificationClass -> every property read returns Error(OBJECT, UNKNOWN_OBJECT)
/// - trendLog, trendLogMultiple, eventLog -> reads Abort
/// - file -> FileAccessMethod is absent and every AtomicReadFile/AtomicWriteFile aborts
/// - networkPort -> no data link behind it
/// - elevatorGroup, lift, escalator -> required object-identifier properties are unset and
/// reads Abort
/// And for these three, call BACnetStack_AddObject FIRST and then the configuration function, or
/// the object exists with no stack data behind it:
/// - channel -> then BACnetStack_AddChannelObject
/// - group -> then BACnetStack_AddGroupObject
/// - schedule -> then BACnetStack_AddScheduleObject
///
/// A DUPLICATE ADD IS A SILENT SUCCESS: if the object already exists this returns true, adds
/// nothing and
/// does not increment Database_Revision. True does not mean "created".
///
/// objectType is NOT validated here, and the type is marked supported - and so appears in the
/// device's
/// Protocol_Object_Types_Supported - BEFORE the duplicate check, so even a no-op call marks the
/// type.
///
/// Object types that need extra configuration have their own constructors
/// (BACnetStack_AddTrendLogObject, BACnetStack_AddFileObject, BACnetStack_AddNetworkPortObject
/// and
/// others); use those instead of this function for those types.
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_AddObject(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const uint16_t
/// objectType, const uint32_t objectInstance)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The device instance of the BACnet device to add the object.
/// * `objectType` - (in) The object type of the BACnet object to add. See
///   #CASBACnetStack::BACnetObjectType for more info.
/// * `objectInstance` - (in) The object instance of the BACnet object to add. Must be between 0 and 4194302
///   inclusively (22 bits). 4194303 is the reserved "unknown instance" value and is
///   rejected.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// True if the object exists on the device after the call - whether this call created it or
/// it was already there. False if the device does not exist or objectInstance is out of
/// range.
pub fn BACnetStack_AddObject(
    deviceInstance: u32,
    objectType: u16,
    objectInstance: u32,
) -> BACnetResult<bool> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32, u16, u32) -> bool> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_AddObject")?;
        Ok(function(deviceInstance, objectType, objectInstance))
    }
}

/// Adds a BACnet Network Port Object. The device must be added first before this function can be
/// called.
///
/// Network_Number and Network_Number_Quality apply ONLY when protocolLevel is BACNET_APPLICATION.
/// Table 12-71.2 marks them required in that column alone, and this function enables them only
/// there - so on a PHYSICAL or PROTOCOL port the networkNumber and networkNumberQuality arguments
/// are accepted and then ignored, and reading either property returns
/// Error(PROPERTY, UNKNOWN_PROPERTY).
///
/// WHICH PROPERTIES EXIST depends on networkType and protocolLevel - ASHRAE 135-2024 Table 12-71
/// plus the sub-table for the network type and the column for the protocol level. The table below
/// covers the common case, networkType = IPV4 with protocolLevel = BACNET_APPLICATION; summaries
/// of
/// the other supported combinations follow it. ARCNET, PTP, LONTALK, ZIGBEE, IPV6 and SERIAL are
/// NOT implemented: this function returns false for them.
///
/// Network Port differs from every other object type in this API in one important way: for most
/// of
/// its properties the stack tries a registered Get callback FIRST and falls back to its own
/// stored
/// value, so an application MAY override a stack-held value. The exceptions - MAC_Address,
/// APDU_Length, FD_BBMD_Address and the BACnet/SC properties - are answered by the stack
/// unconditionally and cannot be overridden.
///
/// Handled and stored by the CAS BACnet Stack (a Get callback, if registered, wins):
/// - Network_Type, Protocol_Level, Reference_Port (all set by this function)
/// - Network_Number, Network_Number_Quality (set by this function)
/// - BACnet_IP_Mode (always NORMAL), Changes_Pending (always FALSE)
/// - Command (always IDLE) and Routing_Table, if you enable them
/// - Object_Identifier, Object_Type, Status_Flags, Property_List
///
/// Generated by the stack and NOT overridable by any callback:
/// - APDU_Length - 1476 for IPV4 / ETHERNET / VIRTUAL / SC, 480 for MSTP
/// - MAC_Address - for IPV4 + BACNET_APPLICATION this is READ-ONLY and derived from IP_Address
/// and BACnet_IP_UDP_Port (cl. 12.56.15), so it is served indirectly by those two callbacks.
/// For MSTP it comes from CallbackGetPropertyOctetString (exactly one octet). For
/// BACnet/SC it comes from stack SC state.
///
/// Callbacks are used to handle the other properties. The following list contains those
/// properties
/// for networkType = IPV4, protocolLevel = BACNET_APPLICATION, and the Callbacks that are used to
/// get the required data:
///
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Property Datatype Callback
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// ObjectName CharacterString CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString
/// CallbackSetPropertyCharacterString
/// (only if made writable)
/// Reliability* Enumerated CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated
/// OutOfService Boolean CallbackGetPropertyBool
/// CallbackSetPropertyBool
/// IPAddress OctetString CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// CallbackSetPropertyOctetString
/// IPSubnetMask OctetString CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// CallbackSetPropertyOctetString
/// IPDefaultGateway OctetString CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// CallbackSetPropertyOctetString
/// IPDNSServer** OctetString array CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// BACnetIPUDPPort Unsigned CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// CallbackSetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// LinkSpeed Real CallbackGetPropertyReal
/// FDBBMDAddress*** BACnetHostNPort CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated
/// CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// CallbackGetPropertyOctetString
/// CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString
/// FDSubscriptionLifetime****
/// Unsigned CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// CallbackSetPropertyUnsignedInteger
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Served by the stack's intrinsic alarm engine instead, if intrinsic reporting has been
/// enabled for this object.
/// * An array: the stack calls CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger with array index 0 for
/// the element count, then CallbackGetPropertyOctetString per element.
/// ** Assembled from several calls, all against propertyIdentifier FD_BBMD_Address:
/// CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated with no array index returns the host type
/// (0 = none, 1 = ip-address, 2 = name); CallbackGetPropertyUnsignedInteger with array
/// index 2 returns the port; then array index 1 returns the host - via
/// CallbackGetPropertyOctetString for an IP address, or
/// CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString for a name. A missing
/// CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated Aborts the read.
/// *** No default: an unanswered read returns Error(PROPERTY, VALUE_NOT_INITIALIZED) rather
/// than a fabricated 0.
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
///
/// Other supported networkType / protocolLevel combinations:
/// - IPV4 + PROTOCOL: as above but without BACnet_IP_Mode, BACnet_IP_UDP_Port, Network_Number,
/// Network_Number_Quality, APDU_Length and the foreign-device properties. PHYSICAL and
/// NON_BACNET_APPLICATION are rejected.
/// - ETHERNET: PHYSICAL only. Reference_Port, MAC_Address, Link_Speed. Every other protocol
/// level is rejected.
/// - MSTP: BACNET_APPLICATION only. Reference_Port, Max_Manager and Max_Info_Frames (made
/// writable if the WriteProperty service is enabled), MAC_Address, Network_Number,
/// Network_Number_Quality, APDU_Length.
/// - VIRTUAL: any protocol level. Reference_Port and MAC_Address always; BACNET_APPLICATION
/// adds Network_Number, Network_Number_Quality, APDU_Length.
/// - SECURE_CONNECT (requires the BACnet/SC data link): BACNET_APPLICATION enables
/// Reference_Port, Out_Of_Service (writable), Network_Number, Network_Number_Quality,
/// APDU_Length, MAC_Address, Max_BVLC_Length_Accepted, Max_NPDU_Length_Accepted,
/// SC_Primary_Hub_URI, SC_Failover_Hub_URI, SC_Minimum_Reconnect_Time,
/// SC_Maximum_Reconnect_Time, SC_Connect_Wait_Timeout, SC_Disconnect_Wait_Timeout,
/// SC_Heartbeat_Timeout (all writable), Operational_Certificate_File,
/// Issuer_Certificate_Files, Certificate_Signing_Request_File and SC_Hub_Connector_State.
/// ALL of these are stored and served by the stack and are NOT callback-overridable.
/// Current_Health and the SC connection-status properties are deliberately left for the
/// application to enable.
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_AddNetworkPortObject(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
/// uint32_t objectInstance, const uint8_t networkType, const uint8_t protocolLevel, const
/// uint16_t networkNumber, const uint8_t networkNumberQuality, const uint32_t referencePort)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The instance number of the device that owns this Network Port object.
/// * `objectInstance` - (in) The instance number of this Network Port object. Must be between 0 and 4194302
///   inclusively (22 bits). 4194303 is the reserved "unknown instance" value and is
///   rejected.
/// * `networkType` - (in) The type of network this Network Port object is representing. See
///   #CASBACnetStack::BACnetNetworkType for available values.
/// * `protocolLevel` - (in) The protocol level for this Network Port object. See
///   #CASBACnetStack::BACnetProtocolLevel for available values.
/// * `networkNumber` - (in) The network number for this Network Port object.
/// * `networkNumberQuality` - (in) The quality of the network number for this Network Port object: 0 = UNKNOWN, 1 =
///   LEARNED, 2 = LEARNED_CONFIGURED, 3 = CONFIGURED. Values above CONFIGURED are rejected.
///   See #CASBACnetStack::BACnetNetworkNumberQuality.
/// * `referencePort` - (in) the instance of the Network Port object that this Network Port object uses as a
///   lower protocol layer. If this is the lowest protocol layer, use 4194303.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// True if successful, false if failed.
pub fn BACnetStack_AddNetworkPortObject(
    deviceInstance: u32,
    objectInstance: u32,
    networkType: u8,
    protocolLevel: u8,
    networkNumber: u16,
    networkNumberQuality: u8,
    referencePort: u32,
) -> BACnetResult<bool> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32, u32, u8, u8, u16, u8, u32) -> bool> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_AddNetworkPortObject")?;
        Ok(function(deviceInstance, objectInstance, networkType, protocolLevel, networkNumber, networkNumberQuality, referencePort))
    }
}

/// Adds a BACnet Trend Log Object. The device must be added first before this function can be
/// called.
/// The Trend Log Object references a logged device, object, and property and when enabled, will
/// log
/// the values of the property based on the logging type. The logging type for this log object is
/// set
/// by calling one of the following functions after calling this function:
/// - SetTrendLogTypeToPolled
/// - SetTrendLogTypeToCOV
/// - SetTrendLogTypeToTriggered
///
/// Users must also pass in the max buffer size which represents the maximum number of records to
/// store.
///
/// The Trend Log properties that are handled and stored by the CAS BACnet Stack are:
/// - Enable, Stop_When_Full, Trigger, Logging_Type
/// - Start_Time, Stop_Time, Log_Interval
/// - Buffer_Size, Record_Count, Total_Record_Count
/// - Log_DeviceObjectProperty (set by this function), Client_COV_Increment
/// - Log_Buffer, Object_Identifier, Object_Type, Status_Flags, Property_List
/// These are answered from stack storage BEFORE any callback is consulted, so a registered Get
/// callback CANNOT override them. Writes to the writable ones land in the same storage, not in a
/// Set callback - and a WriteProperty to ANY Trend Log property Aborts unless
/// BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackGetSystemTime has been called.
/// Log_Buffer is readable only with ReadRange; a ReadProperty of it returns
/// Error(OBJECT, READ_ACCESS_DENIED).
///
/// Callbacks are used to handle the remaining required properties. The following list contains
/// those properties and the Callbacks that are used to get the required data:
///
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Property Datatype Callback
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// ObjectName CharacterString CallbackGetPropertyCharacterString
/// CallbackSetPropertyCharacterString
/// (only if made writable)
/// EventState* Enumerated CallbackGetPropertyEnumerated
/// Served by the stack's intrinsic alarm engine instead, if intrinsic reporting has been
/// enabled for this object. With neither an alarm engine nor a callback the stack
/// answers NORMAL.
/// --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_AddTrendLogObject(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
/// uint32_t objectInstance, const uint16_t logObjectType, const uint32_t logObjectInstance, const
/// uint32_t logPropertyIdentifier, const bool useLogPropertyArrayIndex, const uint32_t
/// logPropertyArrayIndex, const uint32_t maxBufferSize, const bool isLoggedObjectInRemoteDevice,
/// const uint32_t logDeviceInstance)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The instance number of the device that owns this Trend Log Object.
/// * `objectInstance` - (in) The instance number of this Trend Log Object.
/// * `logObjectType` - (in) The BACnet object type of the object to log. See
///   #CASBACnetStack::BACnetObjectType for available values.
/// * `logObjectInstance` - (in) The instance number of the object to log.
/// * `logPropertyIdentifier` - (in) The BACnet Property Identifier of the object to log. See
///   #BACnetPropertyIdentifier for available values.
/// * `useLogPropertyArrayIndex` - (in) Flag that determines whether to use logPropertyArrayIndex (true) or log the whole
///   property (false). #964: lets a Trend Log record one array element - e.g.
///   Priority_Array[8] of a commandable object, or one element of a Multi-state object's
///   State_Text - instead of only ever the whole property.
/// * `logPropertyArrayIndex` - (in) The property array index to use; ignored when useLogPropertyArrayIndex is false.
/// * `maxBufferSize` - (in) The maximum number of logged records held in memory, i.e. the object's
///   Buffer_Size property. The buffer is circular: once full, the oldest record is
///   overwritten unless Stop_When_Full is set. Each record costs memory proportional to the
///   logged datatype, so size this against the target's RAM rather than picking a large
///   round number. Must be at least 1.
/// * `isLoggedObjectInRemoteDevice` - (in) MUST be false. Passing true ALWAYS fails this call - logging a property held in a
///   remote device is not implemented. Contact Chipkin if you need it.
/// * `logDeviceInstance` - (in) Ignored. It is only consulted on the remote-device path, which cannot succeed
///   (see isLoggedObjectInRemoteDevice). Pass 0.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// True if successful, false if failed.
pub fn BACnetStack_AddTrendLogObject(
    deviceInstance: u32,
    objectInstance: u32,
    logObjectType: u16,
    logObjectInstance: u32,
    logPropertyIdentifier: u32,
    useLogPropertyArrayIndex: bool,
    logPropertyArrayIndex: u32,
    maxBufferSize: u32,
    isLoggedObjectInRemoteDevice: bool,
    logDeviceInstance: u32,
) -> BACnetResult<bool> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32, u32, u16, u32, u32, bool, u32, u32, bool, u32) -> bool> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_AddTrendLogObject")?;
        Ok(function(deviceInstance, objectInstance, logObjectType, logObjectInstance, logPropertyIdentifier, useLogPropertyArrayIndex, logPropertyArrayIndex, maxBufferSize, isLoggedObjectInRemoteDevice, logDeviceInstance))
    }
}

/// Adds a BACnet Trend Log Multiple Object. The device must be added first before this function
