/// Enables segmentation and sets up the various parameters used by the segmentation logic.
/// Also sets up the properties used by the device for segmentation
///
/// RANGES BELOW ARE ENFORCED; an out-of-range value returns false and changes nothing.
/// maxSegmentsAccepted must be 1 (0 is rejected). proposedWindowSize and actualWindowSize must
/// each be 1..127 (MIN_WINDOW_SIZE..MAX_WINDOW_SIZE); 0 is rejected. apduSegmentTimeout must be
/// non-zero (cl. 12.11.27; issue #1015) - 0 is rejected unconditionally, not only when
/// Number_Of_APDU_Retries is non-zero, since no legitimate configuration needs a zero segment
/// timeout. maxSegmentsTransmittable is still NOT validated - any value, including 0, is
/// accepted.
///
/// apduSegmentTimeout is in MILLISECONDS, as cl. 12.11.27 defines APDU_Segment_Timeout, and is
/// advertised over the wire in those units. The spec-suggested default of 5000 means five
/// seconds. (Before this was fixed the internal segment timer added the raw millisecond count
/// to CallbackGetSystemTime(), which is in SECONDS, so 5000 produced a 5000-second timeout and
/// segmentation effectively never timed out. The timer now converts. Because that clock has
/// one-second resolution the conversion rounds UP with a one-second floor, so a sub-second
/// timeout behaves as one second rather than as already-expired.)
///
/// If you were compensating for the old behaviour by passing a small number - 5 to mean five
/// seconds - that now means 5 ms, which the floor turns into one second. Pass 5000.
///
/// The trailing `enabled` argument turns segmentation OFF when false: Segmentation_Supported
/// becomes noSegmentation, Max_Segments_Accepted is reset to 1, and the Max_Segments_Accepted
/// and APDU_Segment_Timeout optional properties are disabled. EVERY OTHER ARGUMENT IS IGNORED
/// when enabled is false, and the call returns true without validating them
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_SetSegmentationEnabled(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
/// uint8_t segmentationSupported, const uint32_t maxSegmentsAccepted, const uint32_t
/// apduSegmentTimeout, const uint8_t proposedWindowSize, const uint8_t actualWindowSize, const
/// uint8_t maxSegmentsTransmittable, const bool enabled)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The instance of the device to enable segmentation
/// * `segmentationSupported` - (in) The segmentation option that the device supports, 0 = SegmentedBoth, 1 =
///   SegmentedTransmit, 2 = SegmentedReceive, 3 = NoSegmentation. ONLY 1
///   (SegmentedTransmit) and 3 (NoSegmentation) are ACCEPTED. 0 and 2 are listed for
///   completeness of the BACnetSegmentation enumeration but are REJECTED - this stack does
///   not implement segmented receive - and the call returns false.
/// * `maxSegmentsAccepted` - (in) If the Segmentation_Supported property of the Device object has a value of
///   SEGMENTED_TRANSMIT or NO_SEGMENTATION, this property shall have a value of 1. If the
///   Segmentation_Supported property of the Device object has a value of SEGMENTED_BOTH or
///   SEGMENTED_RECEIVE, then the value of this property shall have a value greater than 1.
/// * `apduSegmentTimeout` - (in) The amount of time in milliseconds between retransmission of an APDU segment. A
///   suggested default value for this property is 5000 milliseconds. This value shall be
///   non-zero if the Device object property called Number_Of_APDU_Retries is non-zero. In
///   order to achieve reliable communication, it is recommended that the values of the
///   APDU_Segment_Timeout properties of the Device objects of all intercommunicating
///   devices should contain the same value.
