/// Enables the FAULT_OUT_OF_RANGE fault algorithm (135-2024 cl. 13.4.7) with REAL limits.
/// Reliability
/// moves to UNDER_RANGE below faultLowLimit and to OVER_RANGE above faultHighLimit, and back to
/// NO_FAULT_DETECTED inside the range. Cl. 13.4.7 defines no time delay and none is applied here
/// - the
/// transition is indicated on the first evaluation that sees the out-of-range value.
/// The object must exist before calling this function.
///
/// This REAL setter is the only FAULT_OUT_OF_RANGE export, but the stack ACCEPTS the algorithm on
/// six
/// object types: analogInput and analogValue (REAL), largeAnalogValue (Double), integerValue
/// (SignedInteger), positiveIntegerValue and accumulator (UnsignedInteger). Cl. 13.4.7 requires
/// all
/// three numeric parameters to share the monitored value's own datatype, so this export - which
/// only
/// carries REAL limits - now (#993) REFUSES to arm on the four non-REAL object types rather than
/// storing REAL limits that would be compared through the monitored datatype (an arbitrary
/// bit-for-bit
/// reinterpretation). There is currently no typed setter for those four object types; use this
/// export
/// only on analogInput and analogValue.
///
/// This function will enable the required properties and store the initial values in memory.
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_SetFaultOutOfRangeAlgorithmReal(const uint32_t
/// deviceInstance, const uint16_t objectType, const uint32_t objectInstance, const float
/// faultLowLimit, const float faultHighLimit, const bool enable)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The instance number of the device that owns this object.
/// * `objectType` - (in) The object type of the object for alarms and events.
/// * `objectInstance` - (in) The object instance of the object for alarms and events.
/// * `faultLowLimit` - (in) The minimum normal value.
/// * `faultHighLimit` - (in) The maximum normal value.
/// * `enable` - (in) Initial inhibit state, inverted - NOT a remove. true arms the algorithm; false
///   leaves it armed but INHIBITED through the object's Event_Algorithm_Inhibit property
///   (or Reliability_Evaluation_Inhibit for a fault algorithm). Both of those properties
///   are made WRITABLE by BACnetStack_SetAlarmsAndEventsForObjectEnabled, so a remote
///   client can change the state afterwards. To genuinely remove an armed algorithm,
///   disable the object with BACnetStack_SetAlarmsAndEventsForObjectEnabled(...,
///   enabled=false). Recommended value: true
///
/// # Returns
///
/// True on success; false if the device or object does not exist, the object type does not
