/// Enables the LifeSafety fault algorithm for a lifeSafetyPoint / lifeSafetyZone.
/// The object must exist before calling this function.
///
/// When the monitored Present_Value is in the faultValues set the Reliability transitions to
/// MULTI_STATE_FAULT, and back to NO_FAULT_DETECTED when it leaves the set (135-2024 cl. 13.4.4
/// conditions (a) and (b)).
///
/// DEVIATION - cl. 13.4.4 also defines pMode and condition (c): while Reliability is
/// MULTI_STATE_FAULT, a change of the life-safety mode must indicate a fresh MULTI_STATE_FAULT
/// transition. This implementation holds no mode at all and never indicates that re-transition.
///
/// Cl. 13.4.4 requires faultValues to share no value with the alarmValues or
/// lifeSafetyAlarmValues
/// passed to BACnetStack_SetIntrinsicChangeOfLifeSafetyAlgorithm for the same object. That is NOT
/// enforced here - an overlapping value is accepted and produces both a fault and an alarm.
///
/// faultValues is copied, so the caller may free it on return, and each call REPLACES the
/// previous
/// list. A maximum of 64 fault values is supported; #995 changed a faultValueCount above that
/// from a
/// silent clamp (entries past the 64th dropped, call still returning true) to an explicit
/// rejection.
/// A NULL faultValues empties the list, and an empty list means the algorithm never faults.
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_SetFaultLifeSafetyAlgorithm(const uint32_t deviceInstance,
/// const uint16_t objectType, const uint32_t objectInstance, const uint32_t* faultValues, const
