/// Registers a callback to handle read file requests against File objects whose
/// File_Access_Method is
/// record access, and allows implementors to respond with whole records.
/// The callback will be called in response to an AtomicReadFile BACnet message.
///
/// DECLARED LIMIT - at most 128 records per AtomicReadFile request.
/// The CAS BACnet Stack carries at most 128 records ('maxRecordCount') and at most 2048 octets of
/// record
/// data ('maxRecordDataLength') in one record-access response. A 'Requested Record Count' larger
/// than 128
/// is clamped to 128 and answered normally; it is not an error and does not abort. The bound is
/// deliberate: the per-record length array and the payload buffer are stack-allocated, and this
/// stack
/// supports embedded targets.
///
/// This is a DECLARED DEVIATION FROM THE STANDARD, not just a test failure, and is stated here so
/// it is
/// not discovered during a BTL run:
/// - ASHRAE 135-2024 cl. 14.1.5 (normative): the responder "shall read ... the number of records
/// specified by 'Requested Record Count'", and permits a short answer only "If the number of
/// remaining
/// octets or records is less than the requested amount". Clamping returns short while records
/// remain,
/// which that clause does not permit.
/// - Where it is observed - ANY 135.1-2023 record-read test that asks for more than 128 records,
/// not
/// just one of them. A BTL preparer scoping to a single test will under-scope:
/// - 9.12.1.1.1 "Reading an Entire File" - requests '(the number of records in the test file)'
/// and
/// expects 'End of File' = TRUE with all of them. This is the read-from-record-0 case.
/// - 9.12.1.1.3 "Reading Data from an Intermediate Point to the End of the File" - requests
/// '(the number of records in the test file - n)' and expects 'End of File' = TRUE with all of
/// them.
/// - 9.12.1.1.6 "Reading Data Past the End of the File" - requests more records than remain and
/// expects all remaining records with 'End of File' = TRUE.
/// In each case a file of more than 128 records returns 128 with 'End Of File' = FALSE instead.
///
/// A PICS/EPICS for a product built on this stack must declare the 128-record-per-request limit
/// for
/// record-access File objects. Applications that must serve larger files should keep files at or
/// below 128
/// records, or use stream access.
///
/// Also unimplemented, on both the record and the stream path: a response too large for a single
/// APDU is
/// not segmented.
///
/// C declaration: `void
/// BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackReadFileRecord(bool(*FPCallbackReadFileRecord)(const uint32_t
/// deviceInstance, const uint32_t fileInstance, const int32_t fileStartRecord, const uint32_t
/// requestedRecordCount, uint8_t* recordData, uint32_t* recordDataLengths, uint32_t*
/// returnedRecordCount, const uint32_t maxRecordCount, const uint32_t maxRecordDataLength, bool*
/// endOfFile, uint32_t* errorCode))`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `FPCallbackReadFileRecord` - (in) The function pointer for the CallbackReadFileRecord function. See
///   #CASBACnetStack::FPCallbackReadFileRecord for more info.
pub fn BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackReadFileRecord(
    FPCallbackReadFileRecord: Option<FPCallbackReadFileRecord>,
) -> BACnetResult<()> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(Option<FPCallbackReadFileRecord>)> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackReadFileRecord")?;
        function(FPCallbackReadFileRecord);
        Ok(())
    }
}

