// ---- Settings Functions ----

/// Generic setter for the stack tunables enumerated in BACnetStackSetting. This one function
/// intentionally covers all of those settings instead of a dedicated Set<Name> export per
/// setting: it
/// keeps the public ABI small and stable (new settings are appended to the enum without adding
/// new
/// exports). It only fits settings whose value is a single uint32_t; a setting that needs a
/// richer
/// argument (e.g. the DAB heartbeat broadcast destination, which takes a connection string) keeps
/// its
/// own dedicated function and is not part of this enum.
///
/// SCOPE (#1012). Settings are per device, in two layers:
///
/// - `deviceInstance == BACNET_STACK_SETTING_ALL_DEVICES` (4194303) writes the PROCESS DEFAULT -
/// the value used by any device that has not set its own, including devices added later.
/// - any other `deviceInstance` must name a device already added with BACnetStack_AddDevice, and
/// writes an override for that device alone.
///
/// An explicit per-device value always wins over the default, whatever it is - a device may
/// deliberately set BACNET_STACK_SETTING_DISABLED to opt out of a cap the default enables.
/// Writing
/// the default never disturbs a device that has set its own value.
///
/// ONE-WAY: there is currently no way to REMOVE a per-device value once set, i.e. to return a
/// device to following the process default. Setting it to the same value the default happens to
/// hold is not the same thing - the device keeps its own copy and stops tracking later changes to
/// the default. If you need a device to follow the default, do not give it an explicit value.
///
/// C declaration: `bool BACnetStack_SetBACnetStackSetting(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
/// uint32_t setting, const uint32_t value)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The device to configure, or BACNET_STACK_SETTING_ALL_DEVICES for the process
///   default. See the migration note on that constant if you are moving from the pre-#1012
///   two-argument form.
/// * `setting` - (in) One of the BACnetStackSetting identifiers.
/// * `value` - (in) The new value; the meaning is documented per setting on the enum. Some settings
///   accept BACNET_STACK_SETTING_DISABLED to mean "no limit / disabled".
///
/// # Returns
///
/// True if the setting was applied; false if the setting id is unknown, or if
/// `deviceInstance` is neither BACNET_STACK_SETTING_ALL_DEVICES nor a device that has been
/// added. Issue #981: no per-setting VALUE range is actually enforced for any of the settings
/// currently defined - none of them have a documented numeric bound to enforce. A future
/// setting that does need one should add its own check and this doc should note it
/// specifically.
pub fn BACnetStack_SetBACnetStackSetting(
    deviceInstance: u32,
    setting: u32,
    value: u32,
) -> BACnetResult<bool> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32, u32, u32) -> bool> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_SetBACnetStackSetting")?;
        Ok(function(deviceInstance, setting, value))
    }
}

/// Reads back a setting previously applied with BACnetStack_SetBACnetStackSetting. Symmetric with
/// the setter and scoped the same way (#1012).
///
/// For a real `deviceInstance` this returns the EFFECTIVE value - that device's own override if
/// it
/// set one, otherwise the process default. Pass BACNET_STACK_SETTING_ALL_DEVICES to read the
/// default itself. Those two can differ, and comparing them is how a caller tells "this device
/// overrides the default" from "this device follows it".
///
/// C declaration: `uint32_t BACnetStack_GetBACnetStackSetting(const uint32_t deviceInstance,
/// const uint32_t setting)`
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `deviceInstance` - (in) The device to query, or BACNET_STACK_SETTING_ALL_DEVICES for the process default.
/// * `setting` - (in) One of the BACnetStackSetting identifiers.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// The effective value of the setting, or BACNET_STACK_SETTING_DISABLED (0xFFFFFFFF) if the
/// setting id is unknown or `deviceInstance` names a device that has not been added. Note
/// that BACNET_STACK_SETTING_DISABLED is also a legitimate value for settings that use it as
/// their "no limit" sentinel, so it does not by itself indicate an error for those settings.
pub fn BACnetStack_GetBACnetStackSetting(deviceInstance: u32, setting: u32) -> BACnetResult<u32> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32, u32) -> u32> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_GetBACnetStackSetting")?;
        Ok(function(deviceInstance, setting))
    }
}

// ---- Helper Stack Functions ----

/// Tears the whole stack down and returns it to its factory-default state. This is the
/// counterpart to
/// the implicit start-up, not a partial reset - it clears ALL of the following:
///
/// - every registered device, object and property;
/// - every callback and hook held in the callback table - all must be registered again.
/// EXCEPTION:
/// the debug-message callback (BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackLogDebugMessage) is stored separately
/// and SURVIVES Reset;
/// - all BACnetStackSetting values, back to the defaults documented on the BACnetStackSetting
/// enum;
/// - all BACnet/SC configuration (UUID, VMAC, hub connector, direct connect, certificate
/// bindings);
/// - the network and datalink layers, and the pending outbound Write-BDT client list;
/// - all outstanding sent-confirmed-request state. In-flight invoke-ids are abandoned and no
/// HookTimeout will fire for them.
///
/// What Reset does NOT clear, despite the name:
/// - the BBMD broadcast-distribution table and the foreign-device table. Clear those explicitly
/// with
/// BACnetStack_ResetBroadcastDistributionTable() and BACnetStack_ResetForeignDeviceTable();
/// - the data link's device type - a stack put into BBMD mode with BACnetStack_SetBBMD is still
/// in
/// BBMD mode after a Reset;
/// - the debug-message callback, as noted above;
/// - anything the HOST owns. Your socket or serial port is untouched.
///
/// After this call the stack will neither send nor receive until the host re-registers at least
/// CallbackSendMessageForPort and CallbackReceiveMessageForPort and re-adds its device and
/// objects. This is not a
/// complete return to first-run state - see the exceptions above.
///
/// Do NOT call this from inside a callback or hook - see the re-entrancy rule in the Callback
/// Registration Functions section above.
///
/// C declaration: `void BACnetStack_Reset()`
pub fn BACnetStack_Reset() -> BACnetResult<()> {
    unsafe {
        let function: libloading::Symbol<unsafe extern "C" fn()> =
            LIBRARY.get(b"BACnetStack_Reset")?;
        function();
        Ok(())
    }
}

// ---- BACnetSC Functions ----

/// Sets the device's BACnet/SC device UUID (135-2024 AB.1.5.3), which identifies this device
/// across
/// VMAC and device-instance changes. It is REQUIRED: an SC data link with no UUID refuses to
/// start.
///
/// SET-ONCE. A second call is REJECTED and returns false; the only way to change it is
/// BACnetStack_Reset. It is also DEVICE-WIDE - the value is applied to EVERY BACnet/SC data link
/// instance, and mirrored into the Device object.
///
/// THE VMAC IS DERIVED FROM THE UUID, not the other way round. The hub-function VMAC is the LAST
/// 6
/// octets of this UUID, with the final octet XORed with 0x01 if those 6 octets would otherwise be
/// all-zero or all-FF. Choose the UUID accordingly.
